what many didn't know is underneath it  all chesley was reeling from  debilitating depression  jada pinkett smith breaks down in tears  on red table talk while discussing the  death of former miss usa chesley crist  who died by suicide after a long battle  with depression she was 30 years old  good to be here it's healing for me to  talk about chesley chesley's mother  april simpkins sat down at the red table  for her first interview after her  daughter's death discussing chesley's  final words and the legacy she left  behind she truly was my best friend  she was the first person i talked to  when i woke up we would literally just  go about our morning  facetiming each other she'd be putting  on her makeup  i'd be getting myself ready at my desk  how's your day what's going on so to not  have that makes mornings  awful for me yeah  but  i don't know that i'm going to get over  the grief i'm trying to accept that reef  and i are going to do life together the  mother of six went on to explain how she  knew chesley was destined for great  things since the moment she was born and  although chesley was successful on tv as  an extra correspondent and always  smiling and bubbly on social media april  says her battle with depression was  something she hid  this was not  her first suicide attempt she'd  attempted suicide before and it was  after that first attempt  that she and i grew very close and  i wanted her to feel comfortable calling  me and she began taking all the right  steps she began seeing a counselor she  was getting good sleep at night she knew  all the things to do april then recalls  the day of the tragedy  sunday mornings chesley knows i go to  work out she was leaving her exercise  class on the morning of january 30th  when she noticed a text message from  chesley first i'm sorry  by the time you get this i won't be  alive anymore  and it makes me even more sad to write  this because i know it will hurt you the  most  i think at that point on your blacked  out  because by the time i read the text an  hour had passed  april shared that she didn't read the  entire text immediately revealing that  she had hurried home to call her husband  and figure out what they had to do but  she went on to read to the ladies at the  table chesley's last words to her and  full i love you mom  and you are my best friend and the  person i've lived for for years  i wish i could stay with you but i  cannot bear the crushing weight of  persistent sadness  hopelessness and loneliness any longer  i've never told you these feelings  because i've never wanted you to worry  and because i hoped they would  eventually change  but i know they never will they follow  me through every accomplishment success  family gathering  friendly dinner i cry almost every day  now like i'm in mourning chesley's  message continued she said that she's  wished for death for years and it's a  weight she didn't want to share with  anyone i love you more than any person  i've ever known  you've done nothing wrong  you've done everything right  i no longer feel like i have any purpose  in life  i don't know if i ever really did  chesley's final text revealed what she  hoped was waiting for her on the other  side of her pain so i will leave and  rejoin god in heaven and hope to find  peace there  i don't want to leave but i genuinely  feel like i have to if i want to escape  my loneliness that feels like it has no  end you are the perfect mom  and i will love you forever  even in death chesley ended her message  by letting her mom know to feel free to  share this message because they should  know how great of a mom she is april  also shared her hope that opening up  about her daughter's passing will  encourage others to be kinder to those  who have mental challenges back in 2018  et spoke with jada about the importance  of covering heavy topics and discussing  mental health at the red table you know  i i really want to  be able to have  it takes practice to have those  difficult conversations  with kindness  and consideration  and it's just something that we have to  continue to practice because if we don't  talk about it  we're not going to get to the other side  we're just going to you know  things are just going to stay  in the cracks and fester  so the more that we can bring these  difficult subjects to light and talk  about it the more we can purify these  subjects and bring them into something  new and so  that's why it's important for me
 

